<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Berestechko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1651">1651</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmelnytsky-uprising">Khmelnytsky Uprising</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Berestechko was fought from 28 June to 10 July 1651 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, during the Khmelnytsky Uprising. The army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a> II Casimir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohdan-khmelnytsky">Bohdan Khmelnytsky</a>'s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> after their ally, the Crimean khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam-iii-giray">Islam III Giray</a>, withdrew from the field and took Khmelnytsky with him. It was one of the largest land battles of seventeenth-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-berestechko.jpg" alt="Battle of Berestechko" width="320" height="193" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vernier · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Beresteczko_1651.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The armies met on a hilly plain south of the Styr River near the town of Berestechko, in the Volhynian Voivodeship, between marshes on one side and a forest on the other. Their lines stretched over seven to eight kilometres. Numbers are uncertain on both sides. Duke Bogusław Radziwiłł put the Crown Army at 80,000, half of them nobles of the levée en masse, while modern historians such as Zbigniew Wójcik reduce it to 60,000–63,000. Estimates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> and Crimean Tatar forces range from 90,000 to 130,000, with the Tatars put at 25,000–30,000.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>On 28 June Polish cavalry under Stefan Czarniecki and others repulsed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> without a decisive result. On 29 June the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> threw all their cavalry at the Tatars and were driven back to their camp, losing 300 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szlachta">szlachta</a>. Prince Jeremi Wiśniowiecki, on 30 June, led 18 cavalry companies into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> right wing, and German mercenaries under Colonel Houwaldt drove the Tatars off the field. Polish artillery fired on the khan's standard, mortally wounding his brother Amurat, and the Tatar forces fled, taking Khmelnytsky and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-vyhovsky">Ivan Vyhovsky</a> hostage as they tried to bring the khan back.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> then fortified a camp by the Pliashivka River and held out for ten days under Colonel Fylon Dzhalaliy and later Ivan Bohun. When Bohun crossed the river on 10 July to attack Stanisław Lanckoroński, the peasants in the camp thought themselves abandoned and fled, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poles">Poles</a> stormed the camp. A rearguard of 200 to 300 Cossacks died defending the crossing. Participants reported 3,000 killed in the retreat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>John II Casimir did not press the pursuit, hampered by rain, hunger, disease and the levée's unwillingness to march into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. Khmelnytsky, released by the khan, raised a new army, and the Treaty of Bila Tserkva of 28 September 1651 cut the registered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossacks</a> from 40,000 to 20,000. The historian Natalia Yakovenko sees the battle as the point where the uprising turned from a civil war into a Polish-Ukrainian war. A memorial church of Saint George was built nearby in 1912–1914.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Berestechko” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Eastern Europe: Islam III Giray allied with the Cossack hetman Bohdan Khmelnytsky in 1648, contributing to victories at Zhovti Vody and Korsun before abandoning him at <b>Berestechko</b> in 1651, and Mehmed IV Giray helped Hetman Ivan Vyhovsky defeat a Russian army at the Battle of Konotop in 1659.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crimean-khanate">Crimean Khanate · Military</a></li></ul></section>
